      "summaryZh": [
        "Today we are releasing Inkling 2 Mini, a fast, powerful model that’s small enough to run on your laptop. Inkling 2 Mini is a looped transformer, running the middle layers of the model three times for every token. This allows the model to apply more reasoning and reach the right answer with fewer tokens, especially on tasks such as agentic coding that depend on reasoning quality. Inkling 2 Mini leads every open-weights model with less than 100B total parameters on benchmarks such as DeepSWE v1.1 (48.2%) and Vals Index v2.2 (46.5%). It runs faster than similarly performing models as well. DeepSWE v1.1SWE-bench Pro v2 Hard (51 tasks)Reported coding scores for models under 100B total parameters. The green line is the frontier with Inkling 2 Mini, the dashed line the frontier without it, and the shading the ground it adds. Decoding speed is a theoretical ceiling at 614 GB/s with BF16 weights and batch size one, including repeated reads across loops. Evaluation setups vary by model. Inkling 2 Mini is a Mixture-of-Experts model with 26.4B total parameters, 3.4B active, and 5.4B weight activations per token to account for looping. Looping allows the model to reason longer without writing its reasoning down, which matters for monitoring: our ability to oversee and understand what the model does by reading its chain of thought (CoT). We measured the impact of looping on both performance and monitorability with Inkling 2 Mini and a research-specific variable-loop variant we are releasing alongside it. The variable-loop model’s no-CoT time horizon improved from 16 seconds at one pass to 37 seconds with three, and its performance on a benchmark of serial reasoning kept improving out to ten loops—twice as many as it was trained with. The model’s chain of thought grew shorter with more loops but stayed legible throughout. The model didn’t trend higher on tests of agentic sabotage as loops were added. We’re excited to offer some of the first models with looped architecture available to researchers. The variable-loop model was used for our monitorability experiments as it allows for cleaner comparisons between different looping depths. Most of Inkling 2 Mini’s weights were trained and are deployed natively in NVFP4, with the last four layers in MXFP8. The total memory footprint of Inkling 2 Mini is ~20GB, allowing it to run on local devices such as consumer laptops and desktop AI computers. Our researchers took Inkling 2 Mini on a flight, where it ran offline on a MacBook Pro and built a playable flight simulator in a few minutes. MacBook Pro (M5 Pro, 48GB) Airplane modeLoading the flight simulator Inkling 2 Mini built…To try the flight simulator, open this page on desktop.Building and flying a flight simulator with Inkling 2 Mini. The timer shows real time; most of the recording plays at 15× speed. Inkling 2 Mini is released with open weights and is available on Tinker today for inference and customization. Capabilities Inkling 2 Mini’s strengths mirror those of Inkling 2 Small: it does best on long-horizon agentic work and is the best among open-weights models of similar size on most tasks we measured. Inkling 2 Mini scores ahead of every comparable model on agentic coding benchmarks such as DeepSWE v1.1 and SWE-bench Pro v2, and is behind only Qwen3.8-27B on others. On PMPP-Hard, a kernel-optimization benchmark, it approaches the score of the much larger Inkling 2 Small. While looping improves the model’s reasoning, it doesn’t enable its weights to store more facts—knowledge is mostly a function of total parameter count. Inkling 2 Mini’s score on AA-Omniscience, a measure of factuality, is in line with other models of the same size. Studying the variable-loop model, we saw that performances on long-context benchmarks improve with more passes, such as GraphWalks BFS and MRCR v2; but knowledge-intensive benchmarks doesn’t improve with more passes, such as AA-Omniscience, SimpleQA Verified, SimpleVQA, and WorldVQA. Inkling 2 Mini overcomes the knowledge gap by efficiently using search and tools. It leads open-weights models of similar size on BrowseComp, DeepSearchQA, and MCP Atlas. It is also strong on knowledge work benchmarks that incorporate search and tool use. Inkling 2 MiniGemma 4 31BMuse GlimmerQwen3.8-27BNemotron 3.5 Super VLModel InfoParams (B)activated / total5.4/26.430.7/30.730.0/30.027.0/27.012.0/124.5Third Party IndexAA Index (v5.0)20.1 (Best reported score in this row)4.46.320.1 (Best reported score in this row)13.6Vals Index (v2.2)46.5% (Best reported score in this row)–––29.8% (Second-best reported score in this row)Coding and AutoresearchFrontierCode v2.0stirrup v0.2.115.1% (Best reported score in this row)0.0%1.5%14.9% (Second-best reported score in this row)6.1%DeepSWE v1.1mini-swe-agent v2.4.648.2% (Best reported score in this row)0.3%4.4%42.2% (Second-best reported score in this row)24.7%Terminal Bench v4.0mini-swe-agent v2.4.65.6% (Best reported score in this row)0.0%0.5%5.6% (Best reported score in this row)0.0%Terminal Bench v2.1Inkling 2 Mini in pi v0.83.071.6% (Second-best reported score in this row)43.4%51.7%73.0% (Best reported score in this row)60.7%SWEBench Pro v2 (Hard)mini-swe-agent v2.4.677.9% (Best reported score in this row)14.2%44.3%73.8% (Second-best reported score in this row)44.1%SWEBench MultilingualInkling 2 Mini with bash only73.7% (Best reported score in this row)43.0%66.9%70.0% (Second-best reported score in this row)67.7%SWEBench Verifiedbash only79.2% (Best reported score in this row)46.1%76.0%77.8% (Second-best reported score in this row)71.1%Vibe Code Bench v1.1openhands (vals)77.9% (Best reported score in this row)––64.9% (Second-best reported score in this row)3.5%PMPP-Hard (AUC, 0–10h)pi v0.83.0455.82 (Best reported score in this row)––262.50 (Second-best reported score in this row)253.59Agentic Search, Tool Use, and Knowledge WorkBrowseCompInkling 2 Mini with compaction79.0% (Best reported score in this row)37.0%63.9%61.6%78.9% (Second-best re