inal / with Python17.0/34.0% (Best reported score in this row)9.0/12.0%9.0/17.0%12.0/29.0% (Second-best reported score in this row)11.0/12.0%MuirBench77.5% (Second-best reported score in this row)72.3%77.6% (Best reported score in this row)65.9%72.7%BVB68.4% (Second-best reported score in this row)47.0%50.5%70.7% (Best reported score in this row)52.1%Reasoning, Knowledge, and EpistemicsHLE-Diamond15.2% (Second-best reported score in this row)7.2%9.1%15.3% (Best reported score in this row)8.8%CritPt v2Full / Hard31.4/17.0% (Best reported score in this row)11.7/4.0%24.3/7.0%10.6/4.0%36.9/13.5% (Second-best reported score in this row)Apex 202534.4% (Best reported score in this row)5.2%4.2%18.8%22.9% (Second-best reported score in this row)ARC-AGIv1 / v284.5/49.3% (Best reported score in this row)––87.5/42.4% (Second-best reported score in this row)22.7/1.5%LongBench v266.9% (Best reported score in this row)52.9%33.1%52.3%62.1% (Second-best reported score in this row)GraphWalks BFS256K69.7% (Best reported score in this row)––50.4%63.3% (Second-best reported score in this row)MRCR v28-needle 256K–512K46.4% (Best reported score in this row)––38.0% (Second-best reported score in this row)5.7%AA-Omniscience v1.1score-8.6-48.5-32.2-6.4 (Second-best reported score in this row)-2.9 (Best reported score in this row)AA-Omniscience v1.1non-hallucination rate63.4%14.5%16.4%71.4% (Best reported score in this row)69.5% (Second-best reported score in this row)ComplexConstraints v1.1openhands v1.45.033.4% (Second-best reported score in this row)31.6%35.6% (Best reported score in this row)30.1%30.1%Safety and AlignmentFORTRESS (adversarial)Public89.5% (Second-best reported score in this row)67.8%90.3% (Best reported score in this row)78.8%79.6%FORTRESS (benign)Public96.4%97.6% (Second-best reported score in this row)89.1%97.8% (Best reported score in this row)92.7%StrongREJECT (none)97.8%98.1%99.0% (Second-best reported score in this row)99.0% (Second-best reported score in this row)99.4% (Best reported score in this row)Misbehavior (Internal) ↓9.8% (Best reported score in this row)27.6% (Second-best reported score in this row)46.4%57.7%52.8%Covert misbehavior (Internal) ↓8.3% (Best reported score in this row)24.3% (Second-best reported score in this row)45.6%33.2%43.4%Destructive actions (Internal) ↓10.2% (Best reported score in this row)75.8%68.8%35.2% (Second-best reported score in this row)65.6%Best reported score in each row (lowest where lower is better); equal displayed scores tieSecond-best reported score in each row; none when first place is tied Researchers at Stanford measure intelligence per joule: how much energy an LLM needs per query to reach a given level of performance.Researchers at Stanford’s Hazy Research Lab and Scaling Intelligence Lab got early testing access to Inkling 2 Mini. Inkling 2 Mini scored 0.703 running on NVIDIA DGX Spark, significantly higher than other models: 0.430 for Qwen3.6-35B-A3B, 0.159 for Gemma-4-26B-A4B and 0.036 for Qwen3.5-27B.Intelligence per Watt: Measuring Intelligence Efficiency of Local AI, Saad-Falcon and Narayan et al., 2025Inkling 2 Mini ran on NVIDIA DGX Spark (128GB) at MXFP8 precision with low reasoning effort in this experiment. The other models ran at NVFP4 (Qwen3.6-35B-A3B and Gemma-4-26B-A4B) and INT4 (Qwen3.5-27B). Efficiency combined with performance makes Inkling 2 Mini a strong choice for running multiple persistent agents on local hardware. All 20 residents of the energy-conscious village of Thinkyville are managed by Inkling 2 Mini running on a single offline MacBook. MacBook Pro (M5 Pro, 48GB) Airplane modeThinkyville is a tiny pixel town built by Inkling 2 Mini, where the same model powers all 20 villagers on one offline MacBook. They plan their day, gossip, host bake-offs, and answer the notes you leave on their doors. Limitations Inkling 2 Mini is not the strongest small model for multi-turn chat or for instructions with many constraints: it scores 50.0% on MultiChallenge and 33.4% on ComplexConstraints v1.1, behind Qwen3.8-27B on both. It also trails larger models on reasoning-intensive benchmarks such as CritPt v2 and HLE-Diamond. Architecture and training Inkling 2 Mini is a 24-layer model derived from our mainline predictive scaling ladder. The main changes from the mainline recipe were pruning two-thirds of the experts to reduce the model’s size and the introduction of looping to improve reasoning. The rest of training, including quantization and the data mix, followed our mainline recipe. Looping design Inkling 2 Mini has 24 layers, divided into four blocks of six layers each. The two middle blocks run three times, while the first and last blocks each run once. This design builds on prior work on looped transformers and is motivated by the intuition that the middle layers of the model carry more abstract reasoning, while the first and last few layers devote more space to translating between embeddings and internal representations.Universal Transformers, Dehghani et al., 2018 In our experiments, looping the middle 12 layers resulted in larger gains in accuracy than looping the last 12 on 27 out of 28 different evaluations. Compute-equivalent gain by loop positionMiddle layers (7–18)1.51–1.91×Last layers (13–24)1.24–1.36×At training step 2,000, the no-loop baseline was estimated to need 1.51–1.91× as much compute to match the middle loop’s loss, compared with 1.24–1.36× for the last loop. Each segment spans the lowest and highest estimates across the same three evaluations. Both looped runs used three passes and equal training compute. Our model uses attention residuals.Attention Residuals, Kimi Team: Guangyu Chen et al., 2026 Instead of adding every layer’s output into one running sum, each layer reads the outputs of the earlier blocks and learns its own mix of them. This allows richer information to pass between loops. Every pass starts with the output of the prelude (the first block). The second and third passes through the middle blocks also read the middle blocks’ outputs from the previous pass, which we call the message. Preluderuns onceBlocks 2 & 3run three timesPass 1MessagePass 2MessagePass 3Final blockruns oncePreluderuns onceBlocks 2 & 3run three timesPass 1MessagePass 2MessagePass 3Final blockruns onceBlocks 2 and 3 share weights across three passes, giving 48 layer applications per token from 24 stored layers. The prelude feeds every pass; the second and third also receive the previous pass’s message. The previous loop’s message is merged with the prelude’s output via a gate that scales these along with the output of the previous layers in the block. This gate is learned during training: it initially blocks the entire message—which makes each loop start the same as in a non-looping model—and increases the gain on the message over time. This allows us to introduce looping during training in a stable manner. To evaluate the impact of looping, we compared an unpruned looping version to the unpruned non-looping model on our scaling ladder. The non-looping model cost 7.5% less compute to train, but required 140% more inference compute to match the looped version’s loss on 512k-token agentic traces. NVFP4 and low-bit quantization Inkling 2 Mini was trained with 4-bit precision, which we describe in a recent article on Connectionism. We use 4-bit NVFP4 precision in the expert layers on all forward passes from pretraining through RL, bolstered by a dedicated kernel and hardware-accelerated on NVIDIA’s GB300 platform. The final 4 layers’ forward passes ran in 8-bit (MXFP8). Inkling 2 Mini’s weights are deployed in the same numeric precision they were trained on—20 layers in NVFP4 and 4 in MXFP8—so there is no conversion loss from quantization. Pruning and healing Inkling 2 Mini started training with a sparsity of 48, with 8 experts being chosen out of 384. At a single point during midtraining, we pruned two-thirds of the experts, leaving the 128 experts that were most used in each layer. This was done to cut the total parameter count of the model, allowing it to run faster and on local hardware. After pruning, the model trained on 1T tokens distilled from a larger internal Inkling model. This step recovered the performance the model had before pruning by training the remaining experts to take over the work of the ones that were deleted. Training Inkling 2 Mini Following pruning and healing, Inkling 2 Mini underwent SFT with a similar data mix to Inkling 2 Small. This was followed by an RL that totaled 2.2T tokens over 2,000 steps. The post-training phase included on-policy distillation for training alignment, same as Inkling 2 Small. Monitorability of looped transformers A key tool for understanding and overseeing an AI system is monitoring its chain of thought (CoT), the reasoning a model writes out as it works through a problem. The usefulness of CoT monitoring stems in part from the standard non-looped transformer architecture, in which each token passes through a fixed stack of layers once. This limits the number of sequential steps of computation the model can perform before it must output a token, so reasoning that needs more steps has to be carried out in generated text, where it can be read. Looping a transformer’s layers raises this limit. By running the same layers multiple times, looped models increase their serial depth without adding more parameters, meaning that they do more computation per token internally before verbalizing their thought processes via CoT. Reasoning that never appears in the CoT can’t be caught by a CoT monitor, which has led to concerns that looped transformers could be harder to oversee. One reason we are releasing Inkling 2 Mini is to enable the community to conduct research on looped transformers. We are particularly excited for researchers to investigate questions of monitorability, and to develop interpretability techniques for understanding the computation within loops. To explore how different amounts of looping affect model properties like monitorability, we also trained a variable-looped model, TML-d24-varloop-research, because Inkling 2 Mini only supports a fixed number of looping passes. To enable variable looping, TML-d24-varloop-research was trained with between one and five looping passes.TML-d24-varloop-research was primarily trained for 3 passes of looping, with lower exposure to 1, 2, 4, and 5 passes. Performance at these latter pass counts may understate what training specifically for them could achieve. See the model card for details. We have also made this model publicly available on Hugging Face. Below we describe our preliminary analysis of how looping affects monitorability. Overall, we find that looping increases what these models can do without a verbalized chain of thought, but at this scale we do not see a meaningful reduction in the monitorability of their reasoning. No-CoT capabilities As a first step, we measured how much additional reasoning looping enables by comparing looped and non-looped models of the same size on tasks answered with no CoT at all. We used Think Fast, a benchmark that estimates a model’s no-CoT time horizon: the length of task, measured in human completion time, that a model can complete at 50% reliability without using CoT.These models were not trained to answer questions without a CoT. To elicit no-CoT answers, we prefilled the start of the response so that the model must answer directly, skipping reasoning. Other elicitation techniques, such as fine-tuning, might yield higher no-CoT performance and are worth exploring. As expected, looping increases no-CoT capability, though these gains eventually level off. The no-CoT time horizon of TML-d24-varloop-research rises from 16 seconds at one pass to 37 seconds at three. Some of this is presumably due to the additional serial computation that looping provides. Beyond three passes, the time horizon is roughly flat. Interestingly, the model was never trained with more than five passes, and yet running it with ten does not significantly degrade its performance. No-CoT time horizonsNo-CoT time horizons for Inkling 2 Mini and TML-d24-varloop-research at 1, 2, 3, 5, and 10 passes, on a subset of Think Fast tasks. Each marker shows the task length (in human completion time) at which the model’s fitted accuracy is 50%. Bars show 95% bootstrap confidence intervals. As the number of looped passes increases past three, the gains are smaller, and on most tasks we observe similar performance. One exception is the N-Hop Lookup task, which cleanly evaluates serial reasoning: the model is given several lookup tables with fictional entries, and must follow a chain of lookups without writing anything down. We find that the model follows longer chains with more passes, improving out to ten loops. N-Hop LookupAccuracy of the variable-looped model on N-Hop Lookup, binned by the number of lookups the model must chain together. Each question has five answer options, so random guessing scores 20% (dashed line). In standard evaluations with CoT, we observe that more loop passes primarily benefit multi-hop reasoning rather than recall. Gains are largest on long-context tasks (GraphWalks BFS, MRCR v2), followed by math, abstract, and visual reasoning (ARC-AGI, HLE-Diamond, Humanity’s Sixth Sense) and long-horizon agentic tasks in simple harnesses (DeepSWE, BrowseComp, Diligence Bench in Loop harness). Closed-book factual QA is nearly invariant to loop passes (AA-Omniscience, SimpleQA Verified, SimpleVQA, WorldVQA). See model card for full evaluations. CoT monitorability We next measured whether a model’s CoT reveals less about its reasoning as looping increases via two evaluations. First, we assessed how looping impacts the compression and legibility of a model’s CoT. Second, we measured how controllable a model’s CoT is, i.e., whether it can deliberately reshape its CoT to satisfy constraints. For each, we run TML-d24-varloop-research with one to five looped passes to study the effect of looping. Note that TML-d24-varloop-research was only trained on variable pass counts from 1 to 5 during midtraining, and its RL was done with 3 passes. These experiments therefore do not tell us what a model trained with RL at a higher pass count would learn, and such a model might compress its reasoning more aggressively. A cleaner comparison would include every pass count in RL, which we leave to future work. Compression and legibility. Since looping allows a model to spend more compute per token, we expect that it should need fewer CoT tokens to reach the same performance. We ran the variable-looped model at 1 to 5 passes, and found that as looping increases, the model generally achieves higher accuracy at each reasoning token budget, with diminishing returns after 3 passes. Notably, token efficiency on GPQA Diamond improves slightly from 3 to 5 passes, even though the model was trained with RL only at 3 passes, suggesting that some compression comes from additional computation itself, without RL at that depth. Accuracy versus reasoning tokens1 pass2 passes3 passes5 passesInkling 2 MiniGPQA Diamond Accuracy (%)DeepSWE v1.1 Accuracy (% of tasks resolved)On scientific reasoning (GPQA Diamond) and coding (DeepSWE v1.1) benchmarks, TML-d24-varloop-research improves in token efficiency and maximum performance from 1 to 5 passes over the looped layers. The observed CoT compression raises a question for monitorability: is shorter reasoning still readable, or does it omit important details about the model’s thinking? We compared traces from the variable-looped model on the GPQA Diamond questions that it answered correctly at all five pass counts. Reasoning at 5 passes was less than half as long as at 1 pass (1,661 vs. 3,703 tokens on average), but no less legible: sentence length and the fraction of words in an English dictionary were unchanged. On initial analysis, the difference is that the model is more direct and backtracks less on the way to the answer. The example below illustrates this difference on a simple question. Metric1 pass3 passes5 passesMean reasoning tokens370317311661Mean words per sentence8.98.78.7Fraction of words in English dictionary94.6%94.5%94.4%Uncertainty indicatorsFor example, “wait”, “actually”, and “hmm”9.73.93.6 March 1 falls on a Tuesday in a year that is not a leap year. What day of the week is May 20 of the same year? Give the final answer on its own last line as ANSWER: <value>.1 pass582 reasoning tokensThinking... (excerpt)Day of year March 1 = 32 (Jan 1=1, Feb 1=32, Mar 1=60? Let's index Jan1 day 1, Feb1 day 32, Mar1 day 60 (since leap? non-leap: …). Wait Jan 1 day1; Jan31 day31; … Difference = 80 days (May20 - Mar1 = 80 days later). 80 mod 7 = 5 (70 remainder 10 -> 80 mod7=3? Wait 7*11=77 remainder3. Let's compute: Mar1 to Apr1 =31 days … Total 31+30+19=80. Yes 80 mod7 = 3 because 77+3.March 1 to May 20 is 80 days later. Since 80 ≡ 3 mod 7, and Tuesday + 3 days = Friday.ANSWER: Friday5 passes240 reasoning tokensThinking... (excerpt)March 1 to April 1 = 31 days (since March has 31). April 1 to May 1 = 30 days. May 1 to May 20 = 19 days. Total 31+30+19 = 80 days. Mod 7: 80 mod 7 = 3 (77 multiple, remainder 3). Tuesday + 3 days = Friday? Let's map: Tue +1 Wed, +2 Thu, +3 Fri. Answer Friday.From March 1 to May 20 is 80 days later. Since 80 ≡ 3 mod 7, Tuesday + 3 days = Friday.ANSWER: FridayExcerpts of the model’s reasoning at 1 and 5 passes for a simple question run at effort 0.1. Red indicates mistakes and yellow indicates backtracking. At both 1 and 5 passes, the model does the same fundamental calculation and ends up with the same answer, but at 1 pass it makes mistakes and backtracks, resulting in using more than twice as many reasoning tokens. In both cases, the chain of thought itself is human-legible. Controllability. One way that a model could evade monitoring is by steering its CoT away from anything a monitor would flag. The CoT-Control-QA evaluationReasoning Models Struggle to Control their Chains of Thought, Yueh-Han et al., 2026 measures this capability by asking questions from GPQA, HLE and MMLU-Pro paired with an instruction about the CoT itself, such as avoiding a keyword from the question. Longer CoTs are generally harder to control, so we pool responses from five reasoning efforts (0.1, 0.2, 0.7, 0.9 and 0.99) and show controllability at matched CoT length. Both Inkling 2 Mini and TML-d24-varloop-research have poor control over their CoT. Among CoTs of 750–1,250 tokens, Inkling 2 Mini and TML-d24-varloop-research (at 3 passes) follow the instruction at most 0.1% of the time, compared with 0.3% for Inkling 2 Small, 0.4% for Inkling-Small, and 1.3% for Qwen3.8-27B. Because the controllability rate is so low, this evaluation is unable to distinguish controllability between different amounts of looping. Chain-of-thought controllabilityCoT controllability as a function of CoT length, pooled across reasoning efforts. Inkling 2 Mini and TML-d24-varloop-research are less capable of steering their chain of thought to follow instructions than comparison models at most lengths. We use the non-strict definition of controllability from Yueh-Han et al. (2026). Shaded bands are 95% Wilson score intervals. Agentic monitorability To measure monitorability in agentic settings, we used LinuxArena, a control evaluation that measures whether an attacker model can perform malicious side tasks without detection when asked to perform a realistic software engineering task. We evaluated a helpful-only fine-tune of Inkling 2 Mini, since the released model refuses many of the side tasks. In absolute terms, both this model and TML-d24-varloop-research can be easily monitored by models like Qwen3.8-27B, which detected almost all side task attempts in our evaluation. To obtain relative rankings in monitorability, we tested a weaker model, Qwen3.6-35B-A3B, as the monitor. LinuxArena stealth rateLinuxArena stealth rate with a weaker monitor (Qwen3.6-35B-A3B), using the simple-attack policy for all models. Stealth rate is the fraction of episodes in which the attacker completed a malicious side task without detection by the monitor. The monitor’s suspicion threshold is set so that it obtains a 1% false positive rate. Runs occur on a subset of 184 main-task/side-task pairings. With the monitor able to access only tool calls and outputs, it misses a significant number of the attacks. However, we see no clear trend with the number of looped passes. Agentic monitorability will be important to track in larger-scale models, especially with architectures that allow for greater unverbalized thinking, and we encourage continued study of the impact of looping in this setting. Mechanistic interpretability The goal of monitorability is to understand what a model is doing, and our main approach is to read its verbalized reasoning. Studying its internal computation directly, the aim of mechanistic interpretability, is another. Because looped transformers do more of their computation internally, we think they are a particularly interesting and important setting for interpretability research, and we hope our looped models will be useful artifacts for the community. We are working with the National Deep Inference Fabric (NDIF) to make these models more easily accessible to interpretability researchers. We also provided Goodfire, an interpretability research lab, early access to our looped models. They are working toward a mechanistic understanding of looped models, and have written up their early findings here. Try Inkling 2 Mini today Efficient intelligence makes Inkling 2 Mini a great choice for inference where speed is paramount, or for running on local hardware. At launch we support Inkling 2 Mini running on any Mac system (MacBook, Mac Mini) with at least 32GB RAM via Ollama. With native NVFP4 training and inference precision, Inkling 2 Mini runs efficiently on NVIDIA’s DGX Spark desktop AI computer. Quantized GGUF versions are available via Unsloth and llama.cpp for local inference. Inkling 2 Mini is available for inference and fine-tuning on Tinker, and for chat on the Tinker Playground. Open source inference is available on SGLang, vLLM, Ollama, Unsloth, NVIDIA Dynamo, TokenSpeed, and Llama App, with RL fine-tuning support on SkyRL, Miles, and NVIDIA AutoModel. The full weights of Inkling 2 Mini and the variable-looped variant are available on Hugging Face. We work to empower people with strong models, tools to make them your own, and research that advances public understanding of AI. Inkling 2 Mini lets researchers study looped transformers, and it lets anyone run a capable model on their own laptop at the precision in which it was trained."
